{
  "status": "pending-independent-ai-review",
  "rows": [
    {
      "key": "simplifying-radicals-variables/kp1",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-24.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "7f15d73e3a63ec334b2e58d1af99f11ee669a4e6bebfb3b4c7b950c6193b23d7",
      "changed": true,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "simplifying-radicals-variables/kp1",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "For a nonnegative variable, the principal square root of an even power halves its exponent: $\\sqrt{x^{2n}}=x^n$. The nonnegative assumption ensures the extracted power is the principal root.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Simplify $\\sqrt{x^{16}}$ for $x\\ge0$.",
            "steps": [
              "Write $x^{16}=(x^8)^2$.",
              "The nonnegative square root is $x^8$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "simplifying-radicals-variables/kp2",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-24.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "0f0b5269ba9ee923f8f021246644ecb8bda39f56d556a4e934be0ed1d79fc82b",
      "changed": true,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "simplifying-radicals-variables/kp2",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "For a nonnegative variable with an odd exponent, split off the largest even power. Take the square root of that even-power factor outside and leave one variable factor inside.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Simplify $\\sqrt{x^{13}}$ for $x\\ge0$.",
            "steps": [
              "Split $x^{13}=x^{12}x$.",
              "The even-power root is $\\sqrt{x^{12}}=x^6$.",
              "The result is $x^6\\sqrt x$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "simplifying-radicals-variables/kp3",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-24.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "887976c82b29ca5f94c091704eb48f6d9517f92a8fab957aaa3f369c1a368729",
      "changed": true,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "simplifying-radicals-variables/kp3",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "Assuming the stated variables are nonnegative, simplify the numeric factor and each variable separately. Extract the largest square factor of the number and the largest even power of each variable. Multiply the extracted parts and retain all unpaired factors inside the radical.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Simplify $\\sqrt{98x^5}$ for $x\\ge0$.",
            "steps": [
              "Factor $98x^5=49\\cdot2\\cdot x^4\\cdot x$.",
              "The square factors give $\\sqrt{49}=7$ and $\\sqrt{x^4}=x^2$.",
              "The remaining factors are $2x$, giving $7x^2\\sqrt{2x}$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "adding-subtracting-radicals/kp1",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-01.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "2d66bc215cfa4917533ea3c772a4d31739f8b5e346ad6617c93700c928fff4ac",
      "changed": false,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "adding-subtracting-radicals/kp1",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "Only like radicals, terms with the same radicand, can be combined by adding or subtracting their coefficients.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Simplify $9\\sqrt{11} - 13\\sqrt{11}$.",
            "steps": [
              "Both terms have the radical $\\sqrt{11}$, so combine the coefficients.",
              "$9 - 13 = -4$.",
              "The result is $-4\\sqrt{11}$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "adding-subtracting-radicals/kp2",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-01.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "2a35634d5696e428a7f3d2c7a39af007048db3a1f19b31970a71736ed6d9eed7",
      "changed": false,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "adding-subtracting-radicals/kp2",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "Reduce each radical to its simplest form before checking whether the radicands match and can be combined.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Simplify $\\sqrt{75} - \\sqrt{12}$.",
            "steps": [
              "Simplify $\\sqrt{75} = \\sqrt{25 \\cdot 3} = 5\\sqrt{3}$.",
              "Simplify $\\sqrt{12} = \\sqrt{4 \\cdot 3} = 2\\sqrt{3}$.",
              "Both terms now share the radicand 3, so subtract coefficients: $5 - 2 = 3$.",
              "The result is $3\\sqrt{3}$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "adding-subtracting-radicals/kp3",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-01.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "d35af0455832c9a808e0f7f6acaeea3e91b7ded9da6b1476a44af73ae6168a18",
      "changed": false,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "adding-subtracting-radicals/kp3",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "With three radical terms, simplify each one first; if they all reduce to the same radicand, combine all the coefficients at once, keeping track of signs.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Simplify $\\sqrt{45} + \\sqrt{80} - \\sqrt{20}$.",
            "steps": [
              "Simplify each term: $\\sqrt{45} = 3\\sqrt{5}$, $\\sqrt{80} = 4\\sqrt{5}$, $\\sqrt{20} = 2\\sqrt{5}$.",
              "All three share the radicand 5, so combine the coefficients: $3 + 4 - 2 = 5$.",
              "The result is $5\\sqrt{5}$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "radical-operations/kp1",
      "source_path": "curated-completion/teach-04.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "80f9fffef5633c1c9e7edc308b9ce6189cabdab058714d722c58120e00a3bc3b",
      "changed": false,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "radical-operations/kp1",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "For nonnegative radicands, multiply square roots by multiplying the radicands under one radical; multiply any coefficients separately.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Simplify $3\\sqrt2\\cdot2\\sqrt8$.",
            "steps": [
              "Multiply the coefficients: $3\\cdot2=6$.",
              "Multiply the radicands: $\\sqrt2\\cdot\\sqrt8=\\sqrt{16}=4$.",
              "Combine the parts: $6\\cdot4=24$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "radical-operations/kp2",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-21.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "daf4aa656a3c780a6df3f46cdafad82e86ce2728a70f8969b8fc67b05927ca27",
      "changed": false,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "radical-operations/kp2",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "To distribute a radical over a sum, multiply it into every term, simplify each product on its own, then combine only the like terms.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Expand and simplify $\\sqrt{3}\\left(\\sqrt{48} + \\sqrt{75} - \\sqrt{11}\\right)$.",
            "steps": [
              "Distribute the radical over the three terms: $\\sqrt3\\cdot\\sqrt{48} + \\sqrt3\\cdot\\sqrt{75} - \\sqrt3\\cdot\\sqrt{11}$.",
              "Combine the first pair under one root: $\\sqrt{3\\cdot48} = \\sqrt{144} = 12$.",
              "Combine the second pair under one root: $\\sqrt{3\\cdot75} = \\sqrt{225} = 15$.",
              "Combine the third pair under one root: $\\sqrt{3\\cdot11} = \\sqrt{33}$, and $33$ has no square factor above one, so the radical stays.",
              "Add the two whole results and keep the radical term separate: the expanded form is $27 - \\sqrt{33}$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "radical-operations/kp3",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-21.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "350458ad874aea8e89397645f49c4d83215e7ce3a203af8b3fa5b173f3bd8c9d",
      "changed": true,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "radical-operations/kp3",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "Multiply two radical binomials by distributing each term of the first across each term of the second. Simplify radical products and combine like terms. For a conjugate pair, the middle terms cancel: $(p+\\sqrt q)(p-\\sqrt q)=p^2-q$.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Expand and simplify $(2+\\sqrt3)(2-\\sqrt3)$.",
            "steps": [
              "Distribute to obtain $4-2\\sqrt3+2\\sqrt3-(\\sqrt3)^2$.",
              "The opposite radical terms cancel, and $(\\sqrt3)^2=3$.",
              "The product is $4-3=1$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dividing-radicals/kp1",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-06.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "e237903f37fa0d405bc955674344da1604f408da85d10a1cf409d414ffca1750",
      "changed": true,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "dividing-radicals/kp1",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "For $a\\ge0$ and $b>0$, combine a quotient of principal square roots as $\\sqrt a/\\sqrt b=\\sqrt{a/b}$. Divide the radicands first, then evaluate the resulting perfect-square root.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Simplify $\\sqrt{200}/\\sqrt2$.",
            "steps": [
              "Combine the quotient under one radical: $\\sqrt{200/2}$.",
              "The quotient is $100$, whose principal root is $10$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dividing-radicals/kp2",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-06.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "9ecc2b747a81287e2a4b3f678c6db1d1b29f15f42fcb5294610d95bfbb921570",
      "changed": true,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "dividing-radicals/kp2",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "For a nonnegative numerator and positive perfect-square denominator, split the root of the fraction into the two principal roots. Evaluate the denominator root and simplify the numerator radical by extracting its largest square factor. Reduce any common numeric factor.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Simplify $\\sqrt{45/64}$.",
            "steps": [
              "Split the root: $\\sqrt{45}/\\sqrt{64}$.",
              "The denominator root is $8$; the numerator is $\\sqrt{9\\cdot5}=3\\sqrt5$.",
              "The simplified result is $3\\sqrt5/8$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dividing-radicals/kp3",
      "source_path": "curated-completion/teach-03.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "135795639e5696ec49d73a97657e6054903931e101a08297c814d6ddb9f03158",
      "changed": false,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "dividing-radicals/kp3",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "When the numerator radicand is nonnegative, the denominator radicand is positive, and the denominator coefficient is nonzero, divide coefficients and radicands separately before simplifying the root.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Simplify $\\frac{8\\sqrt{45}}{4\\sqrt5}$.",
            "steps": [
              "Divide the coefficients: $\\frac84=2$.",
              "Divide the radicands: $\\frac{\\sqrt{45}}{\\sqrt5}=\\sqrt{\\frac{45}{5}}=\\sqrt9=3$.",
              "Multiply the two parts: $2\\cdot3=6$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "rationalizing-denominators/kp1",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-22.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "49918be12320bcf43aab6dab0e382efab878722af2d19ed8b28bb25ed48c25e5",
      "changed": false,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "rationalizing-denominators/kp1",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "To rationalize a single-radical denominator, multiply the top and bottom by that same radical.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Rationalize $\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{11}}$.",
            "steps": [
              "Multiply top and bottom by $\\sqrt{11}$: $\\frac{8\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{11}\\cdot\\sqrt{11}}$.",
              "$\\sqrt{11}\\cdot\\sqrt{11} = 11$.",
              "Result: $\\frac{8\\sqrt{11}}{11}$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "rationalizing-denominators/kp2",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-22.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "177475a4455687ebd32080a06313b52b443f4c9ef184c70295047d07ee6d71b1",
      "changed": false,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "rationalizing-denominators/kp2",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "Reduce the radical in the denominator first to keep the numbers small, then rationalize.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Rationalize and simplify $\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{18}}$.",
            "steps": [
              "Reduce the radical: $\\sqrt{18} = \\sqrt{9\\cdot2} = 3\\sqrt2$.",
              "Rewrite: $\\frac{9}{3\\sqrt2} = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt2}$.",
              "Multiply top and bottom by $\\sqrt2$: $\\frac{3\\sqrt2}{2}$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "rationalizing-denominators/kp3",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-22.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "8720ef8a33bbfb45d27643447e581cc92f65c2baa039ed3e786a27345c52a0d6",
      "changed": false,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "rationalizing-denominators/kp3",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "Multiply the top and bottom by the conjugate of a two-term denominator so the denominator becomes an integer.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Rationalize $\\frac{5}{3-\\sqrt7}$.",
            "steps": [
              "Multiply top and bottom by the conjugate $3+\\sqrt7$.",
              "Denominator: $(3-\\sqrt7)(3+\\sqrt7) = 9 - 7 = 2$.",
              "Numerator: $5(3+\\sqrt7) = 15+5\\sqrt7$.",
              "Result: $\\frac{15+5\\sqrt7}{2}$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "radical-exponent-conversion/kp1",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-21.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "de82dfe286a6680dbf017d88334f41cb3aa2a9787a7e7c60882eee1dc5fe304f",
      "changed": true,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "radical-exponent-conversion/kp1",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "The exponent $1/n$ represents an $n$th root: $a^{1/n}=\\sqrt[n]{a}$. Convert a root to a power using its index as the exponent denominator, or read that denominator to identify the root index. For even roots the radicand must be nonnegative in real-number work.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Write $\\sqrt[4]{y}$ as a power of $y$, for $y\\ge0$.",
            "steps": [
              "The root index is $4$.",
              "Use exponent $1/4$: $\\sqrt[4]{y}=y^{1/4}$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "radical-exponent-conversion/kp2",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-21.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "cd951a3765cfed6d7b7fab607029840c1ecb0c96088e714dbaa2dfed0ab87967",
      "changed": true,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "radical-exponent-conversion/kp2",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "For a positive base, $\\sqrt[n]{a^m}=a^{m/n}$. The numerator tells the power and the denominator tells the root index. Read the relationship in either direction to convert notation or identify a missing power or index.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Write $\\sqrt[3]{x^2}$ as a single power of $x$, for $x>0$.",
            "steps": [
              "The power inside is $2$ and the root index is $3$.",
              "Place the power over the index: $x^{2/3}$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "radical-exponent-conversion/kp3",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-21.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "65b623c75cde8dce419f7b6e03be0de4882e70cc31c1dfb7f0d98be03613f2fe",
      "changed": false,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "radical-exponent-conversion/kp3",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "A unit fractional power $a^{1/n}$ equals the nth root of $a$; compute it for a perfect nth power.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Compute $32^{1/5}$.",
            "steps": [
              "$32^{1/5}$ is the fifth root of 32.",
              "$32 = 2^5$, a perfect fifth power.",
              "So $32^{1/5} = 2$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "rational-exponents/kp1",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-22.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "bf696f9106177d500298d638af276ed6adfd43cce95829cb92b6243c99745f41",
      "changed": false,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "rational-exponents/kp1",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "For a fractional exponent $a^{m/n}$ with a perfect nth power, take the nth root first, then raise to the mth power.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Compute $32^{2/5}$.",
            "steps": [
              "Take the fifth root first: $32^{1/5} = 2$, since $2^5 = 32$.",
              "Raise the result to the second power: $2^2 = 4$.",
              "So $32^{2/5} = 4$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "rational-exponents/kp2",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-22.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "6e8fa2b65fc34ca9b593b73660b92f09ee02e7bf563773d9a3a5db3fc6e1249a",
      "changed": false,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "rational-exponents/kp2",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "For a negative fractional exponent, rewrite as the reciprocal of the positive-exponent value, then evaluate.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Compute $16^{-3/4}$.",
            "steps": [
              "Rewrite as a reciprocal: $16^{-3/4} = \\frac{1}{16^{3/4}}$.",
              "Take the fourth root first: $16^{1/4} = 2$.",
              "Raise to the third power: $2^3 = 8$.",
              "So $16^{-3/4} = \\frac18$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "rational-exponents/kp3",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-22.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "a2fb987dfab998e06f47bfac9fba216ad62f131b37fd4cf86eb2ec96130cc206",
      "changed": true,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "rational-exponents/kp3",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "For positive bases, the familiar exponent rules also apply to rational exponents: add them for products of the same base, subtract them for quotients, and multiply them for a power of a power. Use exact fraction arithmetic and reduce the final exponent.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "Simplify $(x^{2/5})^{5/2}$ for $x>0$.",
            "steps": [
              "Multiply the exponents: $(2/5)(5/2)=1$.",
              "The expression is $x^1=x$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "pythagorean-theorem/kp1",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-20.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "673161527b130fe0c715a7ecb50ad5854e39551cd81e45878d7bbd6c08202213",
      "changed": true,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "pythagorean-theorem/kp1",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "In a right triangle, the hypotenuse is opposite the right angle and is the longest side. Its square is the sum of the squares of the legs: $c^2=a^2+b^2$. Add those squares and take the positive square root to find the length.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "A right triangle has legs $20$ and $21$. Find its hypotenuse.",
            "steps": [
              "Use $c=\\sqrt{20^2+21^2}$.",
              "Compute $400+441=841$.",
              "Since $29^2=841$, the hypotenuse is $29$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "pythagorean-theorem/kp2",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-20.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "d09b64e60c38ae883e9b60bc76eb076712a9d12ed7bc27bfafc1a4a86667178c",
      "changed": true,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "pythagorean-theorem/kp2",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "Identify the hypotenuse $c$ and the known leg $a$. Rearrange $a^2+b^2=c^2$ to $b^2=c^2-a^2$. Subtract the known leg square from the hypotenuse square and take the positive root for the missing length.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "A right triangle has hypotenuse $37$ and one leg $12$. Find the other leg.",
            "steps": [
              "Use $b=\\sqrt{37^2-12^2}$.",
              "Compute $1369-144=1225$.",
              "Since $35^2=1225$, the missing leg is $35$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "pythagorean-theorem/kp3",
      "source_path": "whole-course-teach/drafts/part-21.json",
      "original_document_sha256": "a370c55eb9397e3e64636dbf6def8a75f921c97d9189c4088ef452341785a2e6",
      "changed": true,
      "document": {
        "kp_id": "pythagorean-theorem/kp3",
        "kind": "teach",
        "arguments": {
          "concept": "Use the Pythagorean theorem to form the square root for the required right-triangle side. When the radicand is not a perfect square, extract its largest square factor and leave the positive length as an exact simplified radical.",
          "worked_example": {
            "problem": "A right triangle has legs $5$ and $7$. Find the exact hypotenuse.",
            "steps": [
              "The hypotenuse is $\\sqrt{5^2+7^2}=\\sqrt{25+49}=\\sqrt{74}$.",
              "Since $74=2\\cdot37$ has no square factor greater than one, the radical is simplified.",
              "The exact length is $\\sqrt{74}$."
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
